WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:06.560
Ending a long-running TV series is never easy,&nbsp;
and many otherwise great shows have been scuppered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:10.960
by an uneven finale. Sometimes, however,&nbsp;
everything comes together perfectly — leaving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:15.920
fans more than satisfied. Here are some TV&nbsp;
shows that managed to stick the landing.

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:20.240
The Americans is about a family of&nbsp;
Soviet deep-cover agents living in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:24.880
suburbs of Washington D.C. Obviously, then,&nbsp;
things were never going to turn out well.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:27.520
Over the course of the series,&nbsp;
the secret identities of Phillip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:31.360
and Elizabeth Jennings are unknown to&nbsp;
everyone but each other, and, later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:37.120
their American daughter Paige. Their FBI agent&nbsp;
neighbor Stan Beeman, however, has his suspicions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:41.840
and in the series finale "START," they're&nbsp;
finally proven true. Although the Jennings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:45.920
manage to flee to Russia, Stan does get a&nbsp;
chance to confront them at gunpoint in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:51.120
tense standoff — one that could easily have been&nbsp;
the end of the road for the Jennings family.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:51.920
"All this time…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:58.240
I would have done anything for you, Phillip."

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:01.360
Hanging over all this, of course,&nbsp;
is the bittersweet knowledge that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:05.360
the Cold War is coming to an end. The&nbsp;
title of the episode itself refers to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:09.840
an actual strategic arms treaty between&nbsp;
the United States and the Soviet Union,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:13.440
one of the most important diplomatic&nbsp;
moments of the late Cold War.

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:17.520
The episode received widespread acclaim,&nbsp;
with particular focus being given to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:21.760
dramatic and nail-biting confrontation&nbsp;
between Stan and the Jennings family.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:25.280
Practically every line in the scene&nbsp;
was masterfully written and performed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:29.360
making it perhaps the most memorable&nbsp;
moment in a series packed full of them.

00:01:30.549 --> 00:01:32.000
Broad City is a sitcom about the real-life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:35.680
friendship between comediennes Ilana&nbsp;
[ill-AH-nuh] Glazer and Abbi Jacobson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:40.320
two young women struggling to make it in New York&nbsp;
City. The whole show is bizarre and wonderful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:44.560
and the finale, named after the show&nbsp;
itself, is one of its most special episodes.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:49.600
In the finale, Abbi is preparing to move to&nbsp;
Boulder, Colorado, for a new job opportunity. She&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:54.480
and Ilana trek around the city in search of one&nbsp;
last bodega breakfast sandwich before the lunch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.480 --> 00:02:00.320
menu goes up at 11 AM. A whole bunch of hilarity&nbsp;
ensues, mostly involving a perfectly functioning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:05.040
toilet they find on the street and a daylight&nbsp;
robbery in the shadow of the Brooklyn Bridge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:09.680
And yeah, it's all a little weird, but that's&nbsp;
Broad City, for you. Finally, Ilana and Abbi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:14.880
throw one last party and part ways for good — but&nbsp;
promise to stay in touch regularly over the phone.

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:19.920
This finale simultaneously wraps up the&nbsp;
story neatly and perfectly captures the zany,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:24.160
heartwarming energy that made Broad City&nbsp;
one of the best shows of the decade.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:27.520
By the time you reach the end, part of&nbsp;
you wants to just flip back to season&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:31.840
1 and start it all over again — which is&nbsp;
exactly the mark of a truly great finale.

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:35.120
The appropriately-named Office episode "Finale"

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:39.040
pivots around Angela Martin's marriage to&nbsp;
salesman-turned-manager Dwight Schrute,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:43.760
also features a seriously meta team&nbsp;
Q&amp;A panel about… well, The Office.

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:48.880
"Do you find that your life is pointless, now&nbsp;
that nobody's actually filming you anymore?"

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:50.340
"Yes."

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:55.760
In the iconic show's final moments, the main&nbsp;
cast gathers in the office to spend a final&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:00.960
evening together. On paper, it's overstuffed even&nbsp;
for a double episode, but after nine seasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:05.600
it honestly feels like it's not enough. You&nbsp;
want every moment to drag on a little longer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:08.320
because the thought of your time&nbsp;
at Dunder Mifflin Scranton coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:11.200
to an end is, well, a little bit heartbreaking.

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:16.000
And so what if it's a little sappy? "Finale"&nbsp;
is a pitch-perfect ending for an outrageous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:20.160
comedy that found its heart — and then some&nbsp;
— along the way. In terms of highlights,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:25.680
everyone loves the Michael Scott appearance, but&nbsp;
the ending is arguably the best moment of all.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:30.240
Perhaps that's the show's greatest accomplishment:&nbsp;
taking a cast of potentially-boring background&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:35.040
characters and bringing them to life — while&nbsp;
lampooning the mundanity of nine-to-five work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:39.440
Everyone is a bit of a star in the end, and&nbsp;
everyone gets just enough of a happy ending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:44.240
to keep the audience happy, too. Ultimately, the&nbsp;
finale more than makes up for the show's final two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:49.200
questionable seasons, and helps make The Office&nbsp;
one of the most rewatchable series of all time.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:54.880
The West Wing stumbled a bit after creator&nbsp;
and head writer Aaron Sorkin departed the show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.880 --> 00:04:00.240
following its fourth season. However, its seventh&nbsp;
and final season is undoubtedly one of The West&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:05.440
Wing's strongest. The season focuses largely on&nbsp;
President Bartlet's final months as president&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:10.720
and the election between Democrat Matt Santos&nbsp;
and Republican Arnie Vinick — and even features&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:15.280
a live presidential debate between the two&nbsp;
candidates that felt bizarrely authentic.

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:20.640
The final episode, "Tomorrow", mostly deals&nbsp;
with Santos' victory and upcoming inauguration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:23.680
the aftermath of Leo McGarry's unexpected passing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:27.920
and the Bartlet administration's fond&nbsp;
memories of the past eight years in office.

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:30.320
"Tomorrow" wasn't just a nostalgia-fest, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:34.160
and its most powerful moment comes&nbsp;
during the episode's final scene.

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:35.040
"What are you thinking about?"

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:41.040
"Tomorrow."

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:46.320
This vision of earnestness and optimism in&nbsp;
politics feels like everything The West Wing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:51.600
had been building towards — and seems even more&nbsp;
necessary today then it did fifteen years ago.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:56.160
Anyone who's seen it will tell you that&nbsp;
Fleabag should never have a third season.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:01.120
That's not to say the show wasn't great, of course&nbsp;
— anything but. The final episode of season two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:05.280
however, wrapped things up so perfectly&nbsp;
that nothing more needs to be said.

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:09.520
In case you're unaware, Fleabag follows star&nbsp;
and creator Phoebe Waller-Bridge through her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:14.320
escapades as a confused, free-spirited&nbsp;
and nameless woman in London. The final&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:18.800
episode is a pitch-perfect farewell to the&nbsp;
show. Ultimately, it ends with a breakup:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:22.880
Fleabag and the priest decide that — despite their&nbsp;
love for each other — their relationship just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:27.840
won't work out. The episode ends with a knowing&nbsp;
wink to the show's fourth-wall-breaking format.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:32.640
It's an achingly beautiful ending with just&nbsp;
the right dash of ambiguity: will Fleabag&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:37.520
return to her old, self-destructive ways, or&nbsp;
will she grow up at last and move forward?

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:41.680
You can't know for sure, but you can&nbsp;
always hope for the latter. At 12 episodes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:46.880
Fleabag is so short that it almost qualifies as&nbsp;
a miniseries, but it uses every minute of screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:52.480
time to devastating effect — and effortlessly&nbsp;
leaves its bizarre, wonderful mark on TV history.

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:55.760
The Wire is the story of a society in crisis;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.760 --> 00:06:00.240
one in which little acts of opportunistic&nbsp;
selfishness incentivize yet more cheating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:05.440
a cycle that ultimately leads to an endlessly&nbsp;
churning wheel of systemic corruption and despair.

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:08.720
The show's final episode,&nbsp;
"-30-," is one of change.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:12.000
Mayor Carcetti [kar-KETT-ee] leaves&nbsp;
for the Governor's mansion. Valchek&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:16.240
replaces Daniels as Commissioner.&nbsp;
McNulty is fired for insubordination,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:20.720
and replaced as the resident problematic cop&nbsp;
by a disillusioned Kima [KEE-muh] Greggs.

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:24.800
Meanwhile, Bubbles gets clean while&nbsp;
Dukie gets hooked. Omar is dead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:29.040
and Michael Lee takes up his mantle.&nbsp;
Marlo walks free, but loses his empire;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:33.280
while Slim Charles and the New Day Co-Op&nbsp;
assume the reins of power on the streets.

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:37.680
But these changes are deceptive. As The Wire&nbsp;
comes to an end, the wheel continues to turn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:41.360
and Baltimore's vicious cycle of crime&nbsp;
and anger has failed to be broken.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:45.440
New players may have stepped up to assume&nbsp;
control, but you don't need to see what happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:50.000
to them — because you've spent the last five&nbsp;
seasons learning that it hardly even matters.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:54.720
In the end, the garbage rolls downhill, and the&nbsp;
neglected poor suffer the real consequences.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:58.960
It's a tale as old as time, but one&nbsp;
that has rarely been so powerfully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:01.360
tragically, and unforgettably told.

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:07.200
Who said only live-action shows can have mighty&nbsp;
endings? The iconic sci-fi series Futurama follows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:11.600
Phillip J. Fry, a down-on-his-luck pizza&nbsp;
delivery driver who's cryogenically frozen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:17.200
by mistake just before New Year's Day 2000. He&nbsp;
wakes up a millennium later, in New New York,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:22.480
a city now dominated by flying cars, robots,&nbsp;
mutants, and aliens. Fry finds work with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:26.880
delivery crew of Planet Express, which takes&nbsp;
him across the galaxy on a series of bizarre&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:32.000
adventures. More than anything else, Futurama&nbsp;
is crass, clever, and genuinely hilarious.

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:34.240
"Bender, honey, we love you."

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:35.360
"Shut up baby, I know it."

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:40.720
Thing is, Futurama also had the ability to slip&nbsp;
around your defenses and actually make you feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:45.520
something. Fans still reference the episodes&nbsp;
"Jurassic Bark" and "The Luck of the Fryrish" with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:50.640
reverence and awe, and its finale might've been&nbsp;
the show's crowning achievement. In "Meanwhile,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:55.200
Fry's misuse of a time-reverting button&nbsp;
designed by Professor Farnsworth results in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:59.840
the world being frozen — except for himself&nbsp;
and Leela. This allows the two to grow old,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:04.880
together and alone. In the end, a malfunction&nbsp;
results in all the events of the entire show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:09.600
thus far being reset, as Fry and Leela&nbsp;
agree to "go around" one more time.

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:13.520
When the finale first aired, Comedy&nbsp;
Central aired the pilot episode again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:17.280
immediately after, which begs the&nbsp;
question… Did Futurama even end?

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:22.320
It's been repeatedly noted by fans and&nbsp;
critics alike that M*A*S*H lasted far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:26.080
longer than the actual Korean War.&nbsp;
Nonetheless, it still felt hard to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:31.520
say goodbye when the 11-season ratings&nbsp;
behemoth finally bid adieu in 1983.

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:34.800
The final episode, appropriately&nbsp;
entitled 'Goodbye, Farewell, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:39.520
Amen,' depicts the final days of the Korean&nbsp;
War through the eyes of its main cast.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:43.920
In the end, a ceasefire is declared, and the&nbsp;
group throws a final party. The next day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:48.560
after Klinger and Soon-Lee's marriage, everyone&nbsp;
says their final goodbyes and departs for home.

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:52.240
The episode was nominated for a whole&nbsp;
heap of Emmys and Golden Globes, but,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:56.960
more importantly, it had the approval of&nbsp;
the masses, too — bringing in a jaw-dropping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.960 --> 00:09:02.480
121.6 million viewers, beating out that&nbsp;
year's Super Bowl. 'Goodbye, Farewell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:06.800
and Amen' remains one of the most watched&nbsp;
broadcasts in the history of television.

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:12.480
Many shows are remembered more for their finales&nbsp;
than for anything else, but that's usually because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:17.440
things have gone very wrong indeed. Not so for&nbsp;
Six Feet Under, however, which follows the Fisher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:21.520
family and their Los Angeles funeral home in&nbsp;
the tumultuous years after the death of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:26.080
family patriarch. The show itself was acclaimed&nbsp;
for its writing and acting throughout its run,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:30.400
but the finale is most fondly remembered&nbsp;
as one of the finest in television history.

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:34.960
The final episode, "Everyone's Waiting", bucks&nbsp;
series tradition by opening with a birth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:39.440
rather than a death: Brenda and the late&nbsp;
Nate's daughter Willa is eight weeks premature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:43.760
and the doctor tells her all they can do is hope&nbsp;
for the best. After checking in with all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:49.040
surviving characters, the episode's final sequence&nbsp;
flashes forward to the deaths of the entire main&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:54.240
cast as Claire drives away from her family home&nbsp;
to begin a new life in New York. It's a beautiful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:58.800
chilling montage that was totally unexpected&nbsp;
when the episode first aired. In hindsight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:03.840
though, it feels like the only satisfying way&nbsp;
to end such a macabre and groundbreaking show.

00:10:04.640 --> 00:10:08.640
It seems fitting that the show that came&nbsp;
closest to true perfection would be the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:13.840
one to best stick its landing. It's also worth&nbsp;
noting that few people really knew how Breaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:18.240
Bad was going to end. That a show could keep&nbsp;
such a passionate fan-base guessing right up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:22.880
until the last moments is a testament to the&nbsp;
impeccable vision of showrunner Vince Gilligan.

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:26.720
Probably an even bigger question than how&nbsp;
Walter White's story would end was what kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:31.120
of catharsis, if any, he'd be permitted.&nbsp;
After all, Walt was both the show's main&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.120 --> 00:10:35.520
character and its most soulless villain. In&nbsp;
the end, though, he got exactly what he wanted:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.520 --> 00:10:39.760
a chance to see his family one last&nbsp;
time, a last-minute rescue for Jesse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:44.320
vengeance for the death of Hank, and one&nbsp;
last moment with his true love: a meth lab.

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:48.080
Having been wounded in a gunfight moments&nbsp;
earlier, Walt collapses onto the floor of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:53.520
the lab as the police close in — undone by his own&nbsp;
machinations, but satisfied with how he went out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:57.360
It's more or less the perfect ending, and&nbsp;
few shows have managed to match it since.

